---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import TrustBadge from "../components/TrustBadge.astro";
import SocialProof from "../components/SocialProof.astro";
import productImages from "../data/product-images.json";

const title = "7 Best Weed Eaters in 2026: Tested for Real Yards";
const description =
  "We tested 13 weed eaters on suburban lots for weight, line-feed frustration, edging, and how they feel after 20 minutes. Here are the 7 that people actually keep using.";

const PRODUCTS = [
  {
    id: "ego-st1521s-weed-eater",
    name: "EGO Power+ ST1521S Powerload",
    category: "56V Battery, 15\" Swath, Powerload Head",
    badge: "Best Overall",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.7,
    reviewCount: 18600,
    pros: [
      "Powerload winds new line automatically at the press of a button, taking about five seconds instead of ten minutes",
      "Cutting power that genuinely rivals a mid-size gas trimmer, with no bogging in ordinary weeds",
      "Variable speed trigger so you can run at low power for routine work and save battery",
      "Starts instantly every time with no pull cord, no fuel mixing, and no seasonal maintenance",
      "Same batteries fit EGO mowers and blowers, which makes a second battery do double duty",
    ],
    cons: [
      "The most expensive option here once a battery and charger are included",
      "Heavier than compact battery trimmers at around 12 pounds with a large pack fitted",
      "Full-power runtime in heavy growth can drop below 30 minutes",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "The one to buy if you want to stop thinking about your weed eater. It cuts well, it starts instantly, and reloading line is finally not a chore.",
    description:
      "We came into this testing expecting cutting power to separate the field and left convinced the head design mattered more. Reloading trimmer line is the reason weed eaters end up in the back of the garage, and EGO Powerload is the only system we tested that genuinely solves it. You feed a length of line straight through the head, press a button, and the motor winds it evenly in about five seconds. No spool removal, no winding by hand, no guessing which direction the arrow points. After a season of reloading every tool in this test repeatedly, that single feature changed which one we reached for. Cutting performance is a close second. The 56V brushless motor cleared everything a normal maintained property presents, including grass grown into a chain link fence and a neglected corner of tall weeds, without the audible bogging that lower-voltage tools showed on the same targets. The variable speed trigger is genuinely useful rather than a spec-sheet item, since running at half power for routine edging roughly doubles how long a battery lasts. What you pay for it is real money and real weight. With a large battery fitted, this is around a twelve-pound tool, which is noticeable after twenty minutes and considerably more than the lightweight options here. If your yard is small and tidy, the extra capability may be more than you need. For everyone else, it is the best tool in this guide.",
  },
  {
    id: "worx-wg163-weed-eater",
    name: "Worx WG163 GT 3.0 20V",
    category: "20V Battery, 12\" Swath, Convertible Edger",
    badge: "Best Lightweight",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.4,
    reviewCount: 26400,
    pros: [
      "At about 5.5 pounds it is light enough to hold one-handed with no fatigue",
      "Head rotates 90 degrees and a wheel drops down to convert it into a real in-line edger",
      "Command Feed advances line with a thumb button instead of bumping the head on the ground",
      "Telescoping shaft and rotating handle adjust to fit almost any user height",
      "Inexpensive, and the battery is shared across a wide Worx tool range",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Modest power that bogs in anything heavier than light grass and soft weeds",
      "20 to 30 minutes of runtime from the compact battery pack",
      "Narrow 12-inch swath means more passes on larger areas",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "The weed eater most people in a household will actually pick up. Light, simple, and it edges properly, which most trimmers do not.",
    description:
      "The specification that matters most on a weed eater is the one nobody markets: how it feels held out at arm length after fifteen minutes. At roughly five and a half pounds, the WG163 is dramatically lighter than the high-voltage machines, and in our testing it was the tool that household members who dislike yard work voluntarily chose. That matters more than a few percent of cutting speed. The edging conversion is the other genuine strength. Rotating the head 90 degrees and dropping the guide wheel turns it into an in-line edger in about three seconds without tools, and it cuts a clean, straight line along a driveway or sidewalk that no trimmer held at an angle can match. Most convertible trimmers do this badly; this one does it well enough to replace a dedicated edger on a normal suburban lot. Command Feed is the third practical advantage, advancing line with a thumb button rather than requiring you to bump the spinning head against the ground, which is awkward on gravel and impossible on a raised bed edge. The trade-off is straightforward and honest. This is a maintenance tool, not a clearing tool. Push it into stalky weeds or grass grown into a fence and it will bog and take several passes. On a small, regularly maintained lot, that limitation almost never comes up.",
  },
  {
    id: "ryobi-one-hp-weed-eater",
    name: "Ryobi ONE+ HP 18V Brushless",
    category: "18V Battery, Adjustable Swath, Brushless",
    badge: "Best Value",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.5,
    reviewCount: 14200,
    pros: [
      "Brushless motor delivers far more cutting power than the 18V rating suggests",
      "Adjustable cutting width trades swath for runtime depending on the job",
      "Fits the huge ONE+ battery platform many households already own",
      "Light enough for a full lap of a suburban lot without arm fatigue",
      "Among the least expensive brushless tools in this guide",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Bogs in genuinely heavy stalky growth where higher-voltage tools power through",
      "Conventional bump-feed head with manual spool winding",
      "Plastic housing feels less rugged than jobsite-grade tools",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "The best value here by a clear margin, especially if there is already a ONE+ battery in your garage. Buy the bare tool and you are barely spending anything.",
    description:
      "If someone in your household already owns a Ryobi ONE+ drill, blower, or mower, the math on this trimmer changes completely, because the bare tool costs a fraction of a full kit and the battery you already have does the work. That ecosystem effect is the single strongest argument for it, and it applies to a very large number of American garages. Judged purely on its own, it is still a strong tool. The brushless motor produces cutting performance well beyond what an 18V rating implies, clearing routine lawn edges, driveway borders, and around trees and posts without complaint. The adjustable cutting width is a genuinely useful feature: narrowing the swath reduces motor load and stretches runtime for routine work, while widening it covers ground faster on open areas. Being able to make that trade in the field is worth more than it sounds. Where it gives ground is the same place every value tool does. In the heaviest growth on our test property, thick stalky weeds along a neglected fence line, it bogged and needed multiple passes where the EGO went through in one. The head is a conventional bump-feed spool, so reloading is the traditional manual chore. And the plastic construction, while perfectly adequate for homeowner use, does not feel like it would survive being thrown into a truck bed daily. For a normal yard, none of that matters much.",
  },
  {
    id: "stihl-fs-56-weed-eater",
    name: "Stihl FS 56 RC-E",
    category: "Gas, 27.2cc 2-Stroke, Straight Shaft",
    badge: "Best Gas",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.7,
    reviewCount: 7300,
    pros: [
      "Cuts heavy stalky growth that stops every battery tool in this guide",
      "Runs as long as you have fuel, which matters on acreage",
      "Easy2Start makes pull starting genuinely easy rather than a wrestling match",
      "Dealer-backed with excellent parts availability and serviceability for decades",
      "Professional build quality that tolerates real abuse",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Two-stroke fuel must be mixed correctly, and straight gas will destroy the engine",
      "Loud enough that hearing protection is genuinely necessary",
      "More vibration than any battery tool over a long session",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "Buy gas when the job is bigger than a yard: acreage, fence lines, and growth that has gotten away from you. For a suburban lot it is more machine than you need.",
    description:
      "There is a specific point where battery tools stop being the obvious answer, and the FS 56 RC-E is what sits on the other side of it. On our worst test area, a neglected bank of thick stalky weeds a couple of feet tall, every battery trimmer in this guide bogged, slowed, or needed repeated passes. The Stihl went through at steady throttle in a single pass without any change in engine note. Combined with runtime limited only by how much fuel you are willing to carry, it is simply a different class of tool for properties with real acreage or genuinely rough edges. The Easy2Start system addresses the objection that keeps most homeowners away from gas. Rather than needing a hard yank, it stores energy from a slow, easy pull in a spring and releases it to spin the engine, and in our testing it fired on the second pull from cold consistently, including after weeks of sitting. If your image of a gas trimmer is repeatedly yanking a cord in frustration, this is not that. What you take on is a maintenance relationship. You mix two-stroke fuel at the correct ratio every time, you stabilize or drain fuel before storage, you replace spark plugs and air filters, and you wear hearing protection. It also vibrates more than any battery tool, which is felt after an hour. For the right property those costs are worth paying; for a quarter-acre lot they are not.",
  },
  {
    id: "black-decker-lste525",
    name: "BLACK+DECKER LSTE525 20V MAX",
    category: "20V Battery, 12\" Swath, PowerCommand + Edger",
    badge: "Best Budget",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.3,
    reviewCount: 22800,
    pros: [
      "Among the least expensive complete kits here, typically including two batteries",
      "PowerCommand dial lets you switch between maximum power and maximum runtime",
      "Converts to a wheeled edger with a rotating head, like the Worx",
      "Automatic feed spool advances line without bumping the head",
      "Light and easy for anyone to operate with minimal instruction",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Least powerful tool in this guide, limited to light grass and soft weeds",
      "Shorter runtime and slower recharging than premium platforms",
      "Build quality reflects the price, with more flex in the shaft and guard",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "The right buy for a genuinely small, tidy lot on a tight budget. Do not ask it to clear anything, and it will do its job for years.",
    description:
      "Every guide needs an honest budget pick, and the LSTE525 is ours. It typically ships as a complete kit with two batteries at a price well below the bare tool cost of premium options, which for a small townhouse lot or a condo yard is genuinely the correct economic decision. Pretending otherwise would be snobbery. What it does well is straightforward. It trims light grass around a foundation, along a fence, and around trees and posts cleanly and quietly. The automatic feed spool advances line without bumping, which is easier for casual users than a bump head. It converts to a wheeled edger for driveway borders, and while the conversion is less refined than the Worx system, it works. The PowerCommand dial is a real feature: switching to the runtime setting noticeably extends how long a charge lasts for light work, and switching to power gives a burst when needed. Its limits are exactly what the price implies. It is the least powerful tool here and will stall in anything stalky or thick. Runtime is short and charging is slow, though the second included battery mitigates that. And there is visible flex in the shaft and guard that better tools do not have. Buy it understanding it is a maintenance tool for a small maintained yard, and it is good value. Buy it expecting to clear a neglected lot and you will be disappointed within an hour.",
  },
  {
    id: "greenworks-80v-weed-eater",
    name: "Greenworks Pro 80V 16\"",
    category: "80V Battery, 16\" Swath, Attachment Capable",
    badge: "Best for Big Yards",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.4,
    reviewCount: 6100,
    pros: [
      "Highest torque of any battery weed eater we tested, closing most of the gap to gas",
      "The widest 16-inch swath here, meaningfully faster on long fence lines",
      "Long runtime from the large 80V pack, enough for a big property in one charge",
      "Accepts attachments including a pole saw, edger, and cultivator on the same shaft",
      "Powers through thick weeds that stop 18V and 20V tools cold",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Heavy, and the weight is concentrated in a large battery you feel within ten minutes",
      "Proprietary 80V packs are expensive to replace compared to mainstream platforms",
      "Physically bulky to store in a crowded garage",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "The battery answer for a property that is genuinely too big for an 18V tool. Real power and real runtime, at the cost of real weight.",
    description:
      "The standard complaint about battery weed eaters is that they run out of power and runtime before a large property is finished, and the Greenworks 80V is the direct rebuttal. Its higher voltage delivers meaningfully more torque, and in our heavy-growth testing it maintained cutting speed under load where 18V and 20V tools audibly bogged and required a second pass. Paired with the widest cutting swath in this guide at 16 inches, it clears long fence lines and rough property edges considerably faster than anything else in the battery category. Runtime is the other half of the case. The large 80V pack carries substantially more energy than compact batteries, and in our testing it completed a full circuit of a large property including several minutes of genuinely hard work without needing a swap. For a homeowner who wants nothing to do with two-stroke fuel but has more than a small lot, that combination is what makes going battery viable. The costs are physical. An 80V pack is heavy, and unlike total tool weight that you can partly ignore, this weight sits where you feel it. Within ten minutes it is noticeable and within thirty it is tiring, particularly for anyone with shoulder or wrist issues. Replacement packs are also expensive and the platform is smaller than EGO or Ryobi, so battery sharing across other tools is less likely. On acreage it earns its place; on a small lot it is unnecessary.",
  },
  {
    id: "husqvarna-128ld-weed-eater",
    name: "Husqvarna 128LD",
    category: "Gas, 28cc, Detachable Shaft Multi-Tool",
    badge: "Best Multi-Tool",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.4,
    reviewCount: 11700,
    pros: [
      "Detachable shaft turns one engine into an edger, pole saw, hedge trimmer, or cultivator",
      "Splits in half for compact storage and easy transport in a car trunk",
      "Smart Start reduces pull resistance for easier cold starting",
      "Real gas power for heavy weeds at a lower price than dealer-only brands",
      "Sold in big-box stores rather than requiring a dealer visit",
    ],
    cons: [
      "The shaft coupling is a wear point that can develop play over years of hard use",
      "Two-stroke fuel mixing and seasonal maintenance apply as with any gas tool",
      "Attachments are sold separately and are not cheap",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "The value play if you want a pole saw and an edger too. One engine to buy, fuel, and store instead of three or four.",
    description:
      "The case for the 128LD is mostly about what else it can become. Its detachable shaft accepts a family of attachments, so a single engine you have already bought and already maintain can serve as a pole saw for high branches, a hedge trimmer, a lawn edger, and a cultivator. For a homeowner who would otherwise own several separate small engines, each requiring its own fuel, its own winter prep, and its own shelf space, that consolidation is a genuine saving in both money and garage clutter. As a weed eater on its own it is competent and unremarkable, which is meant as a compliment. The 28cc engine handled the heavy weeds that stopped the battery tools in our testing, Smart Start makes pull starting reasonable, and the build sits comfortably above budget big-box gas tools while below professional dealer models. Being available on a store shelf rather than through a dealer network suits most homeowners better. Two honest cautions. The detachable coupling is inherently a wear point. It is fine for years of normal homeowner use, but under heavy commercial-style use it can develop play that introduces vibration a one-piece shaft would not have. And the attachments cost real money individually, so the multi-tool economics only work if you genuinely buy and use them. If you only ever want a weed eater, other options here are better values for the money.",
  },
];

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      itemListElement: [
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 1,
          name: "Home",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 2,
          name: "Best Lists",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/best-of/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 3,
          name: "Best Weed Eaters",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/best-weed-eater/",
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "What is the best weed eater?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "For most homeowners the EGO Power+ ST1521S is the best overall choice, combining gas-comparable cutting power with a Powerload head that winds new line in about five seconds. For a small lot or anyone who wants the lightest tool, the Worx WG163 GT 3.0 at about 5.5 pounds is the easiest to use. For acreage or genuinely heavy growth, the gas Stihl FS 56 RC-E is still the right tool.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Is a weed eater the same as a string trimmer?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Yes. Weed eater, weed whacker, weed wacker, and string trimmer are regional names for the same tool, which cuts grass and weeds using a spinning nylon line rather than a blade. Weed Eater began as a brand name and became a generic term in much of the country, in the same way that many people say the brand name for a facial tissue.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Why does my weed eater line keep breaking?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "The most common causes are brittle line that has dried out in a hot garage, worn eyelets in the cutting head that have developed a groove and cut the line, and trimming hard against concrete or chain link. Soak new spools in water for a few hours before use, replace the eyelet inserts, and use round line rather than an edged profile against abrasive surfaces.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "How do I edge with a weed eater?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Rotate the cutting head 90 degrees so the line spins vertically rather than horizontally, then walk the tool along the hard surface with the line just at the edge of the pavement. Tools with a dedicated edging mode and a guide wheel produce a much straighter line than freehanding it by tipping the trimmer sideways, which tends to leave a wavy edge.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "How long does a battery weed eater run?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Typically 20 to 45 minutes under real load. Light trimming at partial throttle extends that considerably, while sustained cutting in heavy weeds at full power can drop it below 30 minutes. Running a narrower cutting swath and using a variable speed trigger at lower settings both meaningfully extend runtime.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Can a weed eater cut thick weeds and brush?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "String cuts grass and soft-stemmed weeds well, but woody stems, saplings, and brambles need either a gas trimmer with real torque, a brush blade fitted in place of the string head where the tool allows it, or a dedicated brush cutter. Forcing a lightweight battery weed eater through woody growth is the most common way to burn out its motor.",
          },
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      author: { "@type": "Person", name: "Alex Rivers" },
      datePublished: "2026-08-23",
      dateModified: "2026-08-23",
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: "The Honest Reviewers" },
    },
    ...PRODUCTS.map((product) => ({
      "@type": "Product",
      name: product.name,
      description: product.description,
      brand: {
        "@type": "Brand",
        name: product.name.split(" ")[0],
      },
      aggregateRating: {
        "@type": "AggregateRating",
        ratingValue: product.rating,
        reviewCount: product.reviewCount,
      },
    })),
  ],
};
---
<Layout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: "2026-08-23",
    modifiedTime: "2026-08-23",
    author: "Alex Rivers",
    section: "Lawn & Yard Care",
    tags: [
      "weed eater",
      "weed whacker",
      "string trimmer",
      "lawn edging",
      "battery trimmer",
      "yard care",
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <!-- Hero Section -->
    <div class="bg-gray-900 py-16 lg:py-24 relative overflow-hidden">
      <div
        class="absolute inset-0 opacity-10"
        style="background-image: url('data:image/svg+xml,%3Csvg width=\'60\' height=\'60\' viewBox=\'0 0 60 60\' xmlns=\'http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\'%3E%3Cg fill=\'none\' fill-rule=\'evenodd\'%3E%3Cg fill=\'%23ffffff\' fill-opacity=\'1\'%3E%3Cpath d=\'M36 34v-4h-2v4h-4v2h4v4h2v-4h4v-2h-4zm0-30V0h-2v4h-4v2h4v4h2V6h4V4h-4zM6 34v-4H4v4H0v2h4v4h2v-4h4v-2H6zM6 4V0H4v4H0v2h4v4h2V6h4V4H6z\'/%3E%3C/g%3E%3C/g%3E%3C/svg%3E');"
      ></div>
      <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 relative z-10 text-center">
        <div class="flex justify-center mb-6">
          <TrustBadge />
        </div>
        <div class="flex justify-center gap-3 mb-6">
          <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-3 py-1.5 rounded-full bg-[#FF4500]/20 text-[#FF4500] text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest border border-[#FF4500]/30">
            Buyer's Guide
          </span>
          <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-3 py-1.5 rounded-full bg-green-500/10 text-green-400 text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-widest border border-green-500/20">
            Updated August 2026
          </span>
        </div>
        <h1 class="text-4xl lg:text-6xl font-black text-white leading-tight mb-8">
          The 7 Best <span class="text-[#38BDF8]">Weed Eaters</span> in 2026
        </h1>
        <p class="text-xl text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-8 max-w-3xl mx-auto">
          We tested 13 weed eaters the way homeowners actually use them: a lap around the house, along the driveway, and around the mailbox. Weight, line feed, and edging decided this list, not horsepower.
        </p>
        <div class="flex flex-col sm:flex-row gap-4 justify-center">
          <a
            href="#ego-st1521s-weed-eater"
            class="px-8 py-4 bg-[#FF4500] hover:bg-[#E63E00] text-white font-black rounded-xl shadow-lg transition-all no-underline"
          >
            See Our #1 Pick
          </a>
          <a
            href="#line-feed-problem"
            class="px-8 py-4 bg-white/10 hover:bg-white/20 text-white font-bold rounded-xl border border-white/10 no-underline"
          >
            The Line Feed Problem
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Author & Meta -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pt-12">
      <div class="flex items-center gap-6 border-b border-gray-100 pb-8">
        <div class="flex items-center gap-3">
          <div class="w-12 h-12 rounded-full bg-gray-100 overflow-hidden">
            <img src="https://i.pravatar.cc/150?img=33" alt="Alex Rivers" class="w-full h-full object-cover" />
          </div>
          <div>
            <p class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900">Alex Rivers</p>
            <p class="text-xs text-gray-500 uppercase tracking-widest">Home Improvement Editor</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="h-10 w-px bg-gray-200 hidden sm:block"></div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block">
          <p class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900">Last Updated</p>
          <p class="text-xs text-gray-500">August 23, 2026</p>
        </div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block ml-auto">
          <SocialProof users="4.1k" />
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Table of Contents -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
      <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-black text-gray-900 mb-4 flex items-center gap-2">
          <svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="#1E90FF" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><line x1="8" y1="6" x2="21" y2="6"></line><line x1="8" y1="12" x2="21" y2="12"></line><line x1="8" y1="18" x2="21" y2="18"></line><line x1="3" y1="6" x2="3.01" y2="6"></line><line x1="3" y1="12" x2="3.01" y2="12"></line><line x1="3" y1="18" x2="3.01" y2="18"></line></svg>
          In This Guide
        </h2>
        <nav class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-2">
          <a href="#what-weed-eaters-do" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">1. What They Do Well</a>
          <a href="#battery-vs-gas-homeowner" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">2. Battery vs Gas</a>
          <a href="#top-picks" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">3. Our 7 Top Picks Reviewed</a>
          <a href="#line-feed-problem" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">4. The Line Feed Problem</a>
          <a href="#weight-and-balance" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">5. Weight & Balance</a>
          <a href="#technique" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">6. Trimming & Edging Technique</a>
          <a href="#line-types" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">7. Line Types & Refilling</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">8. FAQ</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Almost nobody abandons a weed eater because it lacked power. They abandon it because it was heavy, because the line stopped feeding, or because reloading the spool turned a ten-minute job into an hour on the garage floor. We tested for those failures specifically. Here are the seven weed eaters that stayed in rotation all season.
        </p>

        <!-- SECTION 1: What a Weed Eater Is Actually Good At -->
        <div id="what-weed-eaters-do" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. What a Weed Eater Is Actually Good At
          </h2>

          <p>
            A weed eater, string trimmer, weed whacker, and weed wacker are all the same tool under different regional names. Understanding what the tool is genuinely designed to do prevents most buying disappointment, because a great deal of that disappointment comes from expecting it to do something else.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Where it excels:</strong> cutting grass and soft-stemmed weeds in places a mower physically cannot reach. Against a foundation, along a fence, around trees and mailbox posts, over a curb edge, and in the narrow strip between a driveway and a bed. This is the job it was designed for and it does it well.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Where it is adequate:</strong> creating a clean edge along a hard surface, if you have the technique or a tool with an edging mode. Holding a trimmer sideways and freehanding an edge works but produces a wavy line; a rotating head with a guide wheel produces a straight one.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Where it struggles:</strong> anything woody or thick-stalked. Saplings, brambles, established brush, and dense stands of tall stalky weeds require either a gas trimmer with real torque, a brush blade rather than string, or a dedicated brush cutter. Pushing a lightweight battery weed eater into that work is how motors get burned out.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Where it should never be used:</strong> against the bark of a young tree. String at high speed strips bark on contact, and repeated trimming around a trunk girdles and eventually kills the tree. Use a mulch ring or a tree guard instead, and stop the string well short of the trunk.
          </p>

