        <div id="battery-vs-gas-homeowner" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Battery vs Gas for a Homeowner
          </h2>

          <p>
            For most people reading this, the honest answer is battery, and the reasoning has changed considerably in the last several product generations.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The Case for Battery</h3>

          <p>
            A battery weed eater starts instantly every single time. There is no fuel to mix, nothing to gum up over winter, no spark plug to foul, no air filter to clean, and no pull cord. It is quiet enough to use at eight on a Saturday morning without becoming the neighbor everyone complains about. And for the work a typical maintained property actually presents, the cutting performance of a modern brushless tool at 40V and above is genuinely equivalent to mid-tier gas.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The Case for Gas</h3>

          <p>
            Gas wins on two axes and only two. The first is runtime: a fuel can gives you unlimited working time, while a battery gives you 20 to 45 minutes and then needs an hour on a charger. The second is torque in heavy growth, where a gas engine holds speed through stalky weeds that make battery motors bog. If your property is measured in acres or your edges have genuinely gotten away from you, gas remains the right answer.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The Deciding Question</h3>

          <p>
            Ask how long your trimming actually takes. If a full lap of your property is under half an hour of trigger time, battery is the better tool and the maintenance savings compound every year. If it is longer than that, or if you regularly cut growth thick enough to make a mower struggle, gas or an 80V battery platform with a spare pack is the honest recommendation.
          </p>

