        <!-- SECTION 4: The Line Feed Problem Nobody Warns You About -->
        <div id="line-feed-problem" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. The Line Feed Problem Nobody Warns You About
          </h2>

          <p>
            More weed eaters are abandoned over line feed frustration than over any performance shortcoming. Here is what actually goes wrong and which head designs avoid it.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Bump Feed</h3>

          <p>
            The most common design. You tap the spinning head on the ground and centrifugal force releases a measured length of line. It is simple and reliable in operation, but reloading means removing the spool, winding fresh line by hand in the correct direction with the layers neat and parallel, and reassembling the head. Done wrong, and it is easy to do wrong, the line jams within minutes. This is the design behind most of the frustration.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Automatic Feed</h3>

          <p>
            The tool senses when line has shortened and advances it without any input from you. Convenient in operation, but it tends to consume line faster than bump feed because it advances whether or not you needed it to, and reloading is still the same manual spool-winding process.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Powered Load Systems</h3>

          <p>
            The genuine solution. You thread a length of line straight through the head and press a button, and the motor winds it evenly and correctly in a few seconds. EGO Powerload is the best-known implementation and it works exactly as advertised. If you trim weekly, this feature alone justifies a price premium.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Fixed-Line Heads</h3>

          <p>
            These abandon the spool entirely and accept individual pre-cut lengths of line that snap into the head. Replacing one takes seconds and nothing can weld or tangle. You do it more often since each piece is short, but the failure mode of a jammed spool disappears completely. Aftermarket fixed-line heads fit many popular trimmers and are a common upgrade for people who have given up on spools.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-amber-50 border border-amber-200 rounded-2xl p-6 my-8">
            <p class="text-sm font-black text-amber-800 mb-2">The Trick That Prevents Most Line Jams</p>
            <p class="text-sm text-amber-700">
              Soak a new spool of trimmer line in water for a few hours before installing it. Nylon dries out and becomes brittle in storage, especially in a hot garage, and brittle line breaks constantly and welds to itself under heat. Hydrated line is more flexible, breaks far less, and feeds properly. Store spools in a sealed bag with a damp sponge and the problem largely disappears.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 5: Weight, Balance, and Why They Decide Everything -->
        <div id="weight-and-balance" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Weight, Balance, and Why They Decide Everything
          </h2>

          <p>
            The specification that determines whether a weed eater gets used is almost never printed prominently on the box. Here is what to check before buying.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Total weight is only half the story.</strong> A weed eater is held out from your body at the end of a long shaft, which multiplies the effective load your arms and shoulders carry. Two tools weighing the same can feel completely different depending on where the mass sits. A machine with the battery mounted near the handle feels dramatically lighter in use than one with weight concentrated at the cutting head.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Test it the right way in the store.</strong> Do not just lift it. Hold it in the operating position, out and slightly to one side, and count to thirty. If your shoulder is complaining at thirty seconds, it will be unbearable at fifteen minutes, and no amount of cutting power compensates.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Look for adjustability.</strong> A telescoping shaft and a rotating auxiliary handle let you position the tool for your height and grip rather than adapting to a fixed geometry. This matters enormously for shorter and taller users, and for anyone sharing the tool across a household with different heights.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Consider a shoulder strap.</strong> Most mid and full-size trimmers accept a harness that transfers weight from your arms to your torso. For anything over about eight pounds used for more than fifteen minutes at a time, a strap changes the experience more than any feature on the tool itself, and it typically costs very little.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Battery size is a weight decision, not just a runtime one.</strong> A larger pack extends runtime and adds weight at the same time. On a small property, a compact battery that runs 20 minutes and keeps the tool light is often the better choice than a large one you carry the whole time to get runtime you never use.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 6: Technique: Trimming, Edging, and Scalping -->
        <div id="technique" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Technique: Trimming, Edging, and Scalping
          </h2>

          <p>
            Weed eaters reward technique more than most yard tools, and a few adjustments produce noticeably better results with the tool you already own.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Cut With the Tip, Not the Whole String</h3>

          <p>
            The string cuts most effectively at its outer end where speed is highest. Burying the whole length of string into thick grass bogs the motor, consumes line rapidly, and produces a ragged cut. Work the tip into the target and let it do the cutting, taking material in layers rather than trying to clear it all in one pass.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Know Which Way the Head Spins</h3>

          <p>
            Most trimmers spin counterclockwise, which throws clippings to the left and cuts most cleanly on the left side of the head. Working right to left along an edge means debris is thrown into the area already cut rather than back over the finished lawn, and the cut quality is better. Reversing that direction is why some people find their trimmer throws clippings all over a clean driveway.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Edging Requires a Different Motion</h3>

          <p>
            Trimming cuts horizontally; edging cuts vertically to create a defined line along a driveway or walkway. Rotating the head 90 degrees, or using a tool with a dedicated edging mode and guide wheel, gives a straight consistent line. Freehanding an edge by tipping the trimmer sideways works but produces a wavy result, and it wears line quickly against concrete.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Do Not Scalp</h3>

          <p>
            The most common technique error is cutting too low against a foundation or fence, taking grass down to bare soil. Scalped strips brown out, take weeks to recover, and invite weeds into the bare ground. Aim to match your mowing height rather than cutting as short as the tool will physically go.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 7: Choosing and Refilling Line Without Frustration -->
        <div id="line-types" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Choosing and Refilling Line Without Frustration
          </h2>

          <p>
            Line is the consumable that determines cut quality, motor load, and how often you stop working. Choosing it well and loading it correctly removes most of the ongoing annoyance of owning a weed eater.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Diameter.</strong> Use what your tool is rated for, typically 0.065 inches for light battery tools and 0.080 to 0.095 for heavier battery and gas models. Thicker line is more durable and cuts heavier growth but increases motor load, reduces runtime, and on smaller tools can cause overheating. It is not a free upgrade.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Shape.</strong> Round line is cheapest and most durable and is right for routine grass. Twisted line runs quieter with less vibration. Square, star, and serrated profiles have actual cutting edges that slice through heavy weeds far more effectively, at the cost of breaking sooner against concrete and fences. Match the profile to what you are cutting rather than buying the most aggressive available.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Winding a spool correctly.</strong> Wind in the direction of the arrow molded into the spool, keep the line under tension so it lies flat, and lay the wraps side by side rather than crossing over each other. Crossed wraps are the single most common cause of a spool that jams after thirty seconds of use. Leave the correct length in the retaining notches before reassembling.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Pre-wound spools.</strong> For most homeowners these are worth the modest premium. They are wound correctly by machine, they eliminate the step where things go wrong, and swapping one takes under a minute. The cost difference across a season is small compared to the frustration avoided.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Keep spare line in the house, not the garage.</strong> Heat and dryness make nylon brittle. A spool stored through a hot summer in an uninsulated garage will break far more often than one kept indoors, and that difference is large enough to be mistaken for a defective tool.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 8: Frequently Asked Questions -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best weed eater?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most homeowners the EGO Power+ ST1521S is the best overall choice, combining gas-comparable cutting power with a Powerload head that winds new line in about five seconds. For a small lot or anyone who wants the lightest tool, the Worx WG163 GT 3.0 at about 5.5 pounds is the easiest to use. For acreage or genuinely heavy growth, the gas Stihl FS 56 RC-E is still the right tool.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a weed eater the same as a string trimmer?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes. Weed eater, weed whacker, weed wacker, and string trimmer are regional names for the same tool, which cuts grass and weeds using a spinning nylon line rather than a blade. Weed Eater began as a brand name and became a generic term in much of the country, in the same way that many people say the brand name for a facial tissue.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Why does my line keep breaking?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                The most common causes are brittle line that has dried out in a hot garage, worn eyelets in the cutting head that have developed a groove and cut the line, and trimming hard against concrete or chain link. Soak new spools in water for a few hours before use, replace the eyelet inserts, and use round line rather than an edged profile against abrasive surfaces.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How do I edge with a weed eater?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Rotate the cutting head 90 degrees so the line spins vertically rather than horizontally, then walk the tool along the hard surface with the line just at the edge of the pavement. Tools with a dedicated edging mode and a guide wheel produce a much straighter line than freehanding it by tipping the trimmer sideways, which tends to leave a wavy edge.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long does a battery weed eater run?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Typically 20 to 45 minutes under real load. Light trimming at partial throttle extends that considerably, while sustained cutting in heavy weeds at full power can drop it below 30 minutes. Running a narrower cutting swath and using a variable speed trigger at lower settings both meaningfully extend runtime.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can it cut thick weeds and brush?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                String cuts grass and soft-stemmed weeds well, but woody stems, saplings, and brambles need either a gas trimmer with real torque, a brush blade fitted in place of the string head where the tool allows it, or a dedicated brush cutter. Forcing a lightweight battery weed eater through woody growth is the most common way to burn out its motor.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes Section -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Common Weed Eater Mistakes
          </h2>

          <p>
            Almost every complaint about a weed eater comes down to one of these four things, and none of them are solved by buying a more powerful tool.
          </p>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Trimming Against a Young Tree Trunk</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Nylon line at full speed strips bark on contact, and repeated trimming around a trunk girdles the tree and eventually kills it. The damage is slow enough that most people never connect the two. Put a mulch ring or a trunk guard around young trees and stop the string well short of the bark.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Storing Line in a Hot Garage</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Nylon dries out and becomes brittle with heat and time, and brittle line snaps constantly and welds to itself on the spool. People replace perfectly good tools over this. Keep spare spools indoors, and soak new line in water for a few hours before installing it.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying on Voltage Instead of Weight</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A powerful high-voltage tool that is tiring to hold gets used less than a lighter one with less cutting power. Unless you regularly cut heavy stalky growth, the extra torque is capability you rarely use, paid for in fatigue you feel every time. Hold the tool in the operating position for thirty seconds before deciding.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Burying the Whole String Into Thick Grass</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The line cuts at its tip where speed is highest. Plunging the full length of string into dense growth bogs the motor, burns through line, and gives a ragged cut. Work the tip in and take the material down in layers instead of trying to clear it in a single pass.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

